1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,020
Plug in your phone at 3 a.m.

2
00:00:02,299 --> 00:00:05,074
Then plug it into the same outlet at 3 p.m.

3
00:00:05,770 --> 00:00:08,127
Same wall, same wires.

4
00:00:08,526 --> 00:00:11,409
But depending on where you live, the power plants

5
00:00:11,409 --> 00:00:13,971
on the other end can be completely different.

6
00:00:14,412 --> 00:00:17,408
At night, maybe a wind farm on the plains.

7
00:00:17,918 --> 00:00:22,504
In the afternoon, solar panels that were dark at 3 a.m.

8
00:00:22,904 --> 00:00:26,845
Stick with me, and you'll see why, in sunny states, the

9
00:00:26,845 --> 00:00:30,427
toughest hour for the grid often isn't the hottest one.

10
00:00:30,752 --> 00:00:33,283
And why, some days, power plants

11
00:00:33,283 --> 00:00:35,814
pay to give their electricity away.

12
00:00:36,211 --> 00:00:38,637
Because the grid isn't one machine.

13
00:00:38,881 --> 00:00:41,052
It's a different machine every hour.

14
00:00:41,494 --> 00:00:45,267
So let's follow one day, hour by hour.

15
00:00:46,101 --> 00:00:47,610
Let's start in the dark.

16
00:00:48,214 --> 00:00:52,371
According to the Energy Information Administration, U.S. electricity use

17
00:00:52,371 --> 00:00:56,527
typically bottoms out around five in the morning, when

18
00:00:56,527 --> 00:01:00,683
most people are asleep and most offices are closed.

19
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,961
But it never drops to zero.

20
00:01:03,286 --> 00:01:07,798
Refrigerators and other always-on devices keep running, and the

21
00:01:07,798 --> 00:01:11,808
EIA calls that steady floor of demand baseload.

22
00:01:12,192 --> 00:01:14,886
The classic baseload plant is nuclear.

23
00:01:15,129 --> 00:01:18,399
It's cheap to run once it's built, so operators try

24
00:01:18,399 --> 00:01:21,341
to keep it at full power around the clock.

25
00:01:21,738 --> 00:01:25,163
In 2025, the average U.S. nuclear plant ran

26
00:01:25,163 --> 00:01:28,588
at 91 percent of its maximum possible output,

27
00:01:28,588 --> 00:01:32,013
higher than any other kind of power plant.

28
00:01:32,965 --> 00:01:36,105
Nuclear supplied about 18 percent of

29
00:01:36,105 --> 00:01:39,246
the country's utility-scale electricity that year.

30
00:01:39,642 --> 00:01:41,291
Now add the wind.

31
00:01:42,103 --> 00:01:45,744
In the middle of the country, the EIA

32
00:01:45,744 --> 00:01:48,930
says land-based wind is usually strongest overnight.

33
00:01:49,318 --> 00:01:51,959
Take the Southwest Power Pool, the grid operator

34
00:01:51,959 --> 00:01:54,601
for a big stretch of the Great Plains.

35
00:01:55,274 --> 00:01:59,957
We ran its 2025 numbers from the EIA's hourly grid data:

36
00:01:59,957 --> 00:02:04,641
on average, wind output equaled about 46 percent of demand at

37
00:02:04,641 --> 00:02:08,474
2 a.m., but only about 31 percent at noon.

38
00:02:09,426 --> 00:02:13,280
And for five straight hours before dawn on

39
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:17,135
December 28, 2025, it equaled about 80 percent.

40
00:02:17,514 --> 00:02:21,816
Some nights there's more wind than anyone needs, so operators

41
00:02:21,816 --> 00:02:25,687
turn turbines down to keep supply matched to demand.

42
00:02:26,517 --> 00:02:30,627
Twelve hours later, the machine looks completely different.

43
00:02:30,952 --> 00:02:35,318
The sun is high, air conditioners are running, and in a

44
00:02:35,318 --> 00:02:39,288
growing number of places, solar is doing the heavy lifting.

45
00:02:39,667 --> 00:02:40,886
Take Texas.

46
00:02:41,269 --> 00:02:46,035
In the summer of 2025, solar plants on the main

47
00:02:46,035 --> 00:02:50,801
Texas grid averaged 24 gigawatts between noon and one p.m.

48
00:02:51,498 --> 00:02:54,342
Two summers earlier, it was 12.

49
00:02:54,723 --> 00:02:56,743
Natural gas made room for it.

50
00:02:57,068 --> 00:03:01,863
Gas supplied half of the Texas grid's midday power in the

51
00:03:01,863 --> 00:03:06,658
summer of 2023, and 37 percent in the summer of 2025.

52
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:09,302
California has gone even further.

53
00:03:09,569 --> 00:03:13,566
Solar now supplies almost half of the grid operator's demand

54
00:03:13,566 --> 00:03:17,564
between eight in the morning and four in the afternoon,

55
00:03:17,564 --> 00:03:21,562
and in the first five months of 2026, it out-generated

56
00:03:21,562 --> 00:03:24,361
natural gas on 82 percent of days.

57
00:03:25,209 --> 00:03:27,310
Now here's the twist I promised.

58
00:03:27,554 --> 00:03:29,452
You'd think the toughest moment for

59
00:03:29,452 --> 00:03:31,351
the grid is the hottest afternoon.

60
00:03:31,734 --> 00:03:34,497
But in places with lots of solar,

61
00:03:34,497 --> 00:03:36,865
it's often the stretch around sunset.

62
00:03:37,262 --> 00:03:41,000
To see why, grid operators look at net load:

63
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,739
total demand minus whatever wind and solar are producing.

64
00:03:45,052 --> 00:03:47,966
It's the part every other power plant has to cover.

65
00:03:48,384 --> 00:03:53,353
One catch: this counts only big solar farms and wind farms.

66
00:03:53,736 --> 00:03:56,500
Rooftop panels don't show up as supply at all.

67
00:03:56,743 --> 00:03:59,553
They just make demand look smaller.

68
00:03:59,947 --> 00:04:02,616
Plot net load over a typical day in

69
00:04:02,616 --> 00:04:05,285
California and it sags deep in the middle,

70
00:04:05,285 --> 00:04:07,621
then shoots up steeply in the evening.

71
00:04:08,225 --> 00:04:10,518
It looks like the outline of a

72
00:04:10,518 --> 00:04:12,811
duck, so it's called the duck curve.

73
00:04:13,508 --> 00:04:15,946
And the EIA says the belly

74
00:04:15,946 --> 00:04:18,384
keeps getting deeper as solar grows.

75
00:04:18,765 --> 00:04:21,636
In spring, when demand is low, the belly can

76
00:04:21,636 --> 00:04:24,187
hold more solar than the grid can use.

77
00:04:24,756 --> 00:04:30,236
In 2024, California's grid operator curtailed 3.4 million megawatt-hours of

78
00:04:30,236 --> 00:04:35,716
wind and solar, and 93 percent of it was solar.

79
00:04:36,095 --> 00:04:37,569
Then comes the neck.

80
00:04:38,173 --> 00:04:41,670
As solar fades around sunset, people get home, cook

81
00:04:41,670 --> 00:04:44,779
dinner and turn on lights and air conditioners.

82
00:04:45,476 --> 00:04:49,563
Everything that isn't the sun has to ramp up fast.

83
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:52,861
One fast-growing answer is a battery.

84
00:04:53,105 --> 00:04:55,256
Charge it with cheap midday solar, then

85
00:04:55,256 --> 00:04:57,099
let it run in the evening.

86
00:04:57,703 --> 00:05:00,484
As the EIA puts it, that flattens

87
00:05:00,484 --> 00:05:03,264
the duck's curve and shortens its neck.

88
00:05:03,654 --> 00:05:05,883
And the scale has changed fast.

89
00:05:06,498 --> 00:05:09,862
California's grid operator reported that in 2025, batteries

90
00:05:09,862 --> 00:05:13,225
in its market delivered about 9,100 megawatts on

91
00:05:13,225 --> 00:05:16,588
average in the hour ending at 8 p.m.

92
00:05:16,982 --> 00:05:20,930
In 2024, it was about 5,700.

93
00:05:21,774 --> 00:05:23,469
Which brings us to price.

94
00:05:24,073 --> 00:05:27,439
On the wholesale market, where power is bought

95
00:05:27,439 --> 00:05:30,806
and sold in bulk, electricity can cost wildly

96
00:05:30,806 --> 00:05:34,173
different amounts from one hour to the next.

97
00:05:34,558 --> 00:05:39,539
Berkeley Lab found that the average wholesale price across the major

98
00:05:39,539 --> 00:05:44,067
U.S. power markets in 2023 was 36 dollars per megawatt-hour.

99
00:05:44,496 --> 00:05:49,505
But that average hides huge swings: in Texas, hourly prices

100
00:05:49,505 --> 00:05:54,514
on some summer evenings topped 500 dollars, and in California,

101
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:58,521
on sunny spring days, they dropped below zero.

102
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:00,005
Below zero.

103
00:06:00,702 --> 00:06:03,361
That's the strange part I promised at the start.

104
00:06:03,686 --> 00:06:05,991
A negative price means sellers are

105
00:06:05,991 --> 00:06:08,295
paying buyers to take their power.

106
00:06:08,695 --> 00:06:10,402
Why would anyone do that?

107
00:06:10,645 --> 00:06:12,636
Some plants can't easily or cheaply

108
00:06:12,636 --> 00:06:14,628
shut down for a short stretch.

109
00:06:15,231 --> 00:06:19,323
Nuclear operators try to run at full power, and big

110
00:06:19,323 --> 00:06:23,416
steam plants face costs every time they stop and restart.

111
00:06:23,804 --> 00:06:26,323
And some wind farms earn a tax

112
00:06:26,323 --> 00:06:28,483
credit for every megawatt-hour they produce.

113
00:06:28,727 --> 00:06:33,324
For a short stretch, paying a little beats shutting down.

114
00:06:33,715 --> 00:06:37,462
In California's day-ahead market, prices were negative about

115
00:06:37,462 --> 00:06:41,210
7 percent of the time in 2025, according

116
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:44,489
to the grid operator's own market monitor.

117
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:48,621
And Berkeley Lab found that in 2023, those

118
00:06:48,621 --> 00:06:52,312
negative prices showed up almost entirely in sunny

119
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:55,542
hours of late spring and early summer.

120
00:06:55,929 --> 00:07:00,338
On the other end are the expensive hours, when the grid

121
00:07:00,338 --> 00:07:04,346
calls on peakers, often gas turbines that run only occasionally.

122
00:07:04,857 --> 00:07:08,613
They cost more to run, but they respond quickly,

123
00:07:08,613 --> 00:07:11,951
which is exactly what that evening ramp needs.

124
00:07:12,794 --> 00:07:14,837
So what does this mean at your house?

125
00:07:15,162 --> 00:07:17,856
Most of us never see wholesale

126
00:07:17,856 --> 00:07:20,549
prices, but your timing still matters.

127
00:07:20,929 --> 00:07:23,907
Most electric vehicle drivers charge overnight at

128
00:07:23,907 --> 00:07:26,885
home, according to the Department of Energy.

129
00:07:27,326 --> 00:07:31,979
And some utilities offer time-of-use rates, where electricity costs

130
00:07:31,979 --> 00:07:36,115
less in certain hours and more in others.

131
00:07:36,508 --> 00:07:39,548
That works because most homes now have smart

132
00:07:39,548 --> 00:07:42,208
meters that record use at least hourly.

133
00:07:42,905 --> 00:07:47,394
In 2022, about 73 percent of U.S. residential

134
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:51,322
meters were smart meters, the EIA reports.

135
00:07:51,722 --> 00:07:54,416
And here's where the old advice breaks down.

136
00:07:55,112 --> 00:07:57,765
On California's wholesale market, some of the cheapest

137
00:07:57,765 --> 00:08:00,418
hours are now in the middle of the

138
00:08:00,418 --> 00:08:02,740
day, not the middle of the night.

139
00:08:03,251 --> 00:08:07,744
So read your own utility's rate plan instead of guessing.

140
00:08:08,137 --> 00:08:10,796
Running the dishwasher or charging the car in

141
00:08:10,796 --> 00:08:13,454
a cheap hour isn't just about your bill.

142
00:08:13,733 --> 00:08:15,881
It helps the grid fill the

143
00:08:15,881 --> 00:08:18,029
duck's belly instead of its neck.

144
00:08:18,864 --> 00:08:19,921
Quick recap.

145
00:08:20,362 --> 00:08:23,637
At 3 a.m., demand is near its low,

146
00:08:23,637 --> 00:08:26,911
nuclear runs flat out, and on the Great

147
00:08:26,911 --> 00:08:29,777
Plains, wind is often at its strongest.

148
00:08:30,159 --> 00:08:33,561
At 3 p.m., solar carries more and more of

149
00:08:33,561 --> 00:08:36,962
the load, sometimes enough to push prices below zero.

150
00:08:37,566 --> 00:08:41,343
Then at sunset comes the steep neck of the duck,

151
00:08:41,343 --> 00:08:44,741
and batteries and gas plants race to cover it.

152
00:08:45,138 --> 00:08:47,030
The grid isn't one machine.

153
00:08:47,309 --> 00:08:49,492
It's a different machine every hour.

154
00:08:50,003 --> 00:08:51,965
And now you know how to read it.

155
00:08:52,358 --> 00:08:54,291
So what does your utility charge

156
00:08:54,291 --> 00:08:56,224
at night versus in the afternoon?

157
00:08:56,468 --> 00:08:59,661
Do you have a time-of-use rate, or one flat price?

158
00:08:59,986 --> 00:09:02,110
Tell me your state in the comments.

159
00:09:02,504 --> 00:09:05,768
If you like seeing the hidden machinery behind everyday

160
00:09:05,768 --> 00:09:08,669
life, subscribe to Other End of the Wire.

161
00:09:09,180 --> 00:09:11,101
Every source behind the numbers in

162
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:13,023
this video is in the description.

163
00:09:13,719 --> 00:09:15,275
Follow the electron.
